{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}
{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>File Uploader</h1>
    <p>
        Rhapsody includes a small, secure file uploader: a fluent PHP class that validates and stores files,
        and a drop-in JavaScript file that adds per-file progress bars to any upload form.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick Start</h2>
    <p>In your controller, build an uploader from the current <code>Request</code>, describe what you accept, and call <code>store()</code> with the name of the form field:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\FileUploader;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

public function upload(Request $request): Response
{
    $result = new FileUploader($request)
        -&gt;images()
        -&gt;maxSize('5MB')
        -&gt;store('documents');

    return $this-&gt;json($result-&gt;toArray(), $result-&gt;ok() ? 200 : 422);
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Files are saved to <code>storage/uploads/</code> by default (created on first use). Each file is renamed to a random
        name, and its extension is derived from the detected MIME type, never from the client's filename.
    </p>

    <h2>Configuration</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><code>images()</code> – JPEG, PNG, GIF and WebP. (SVG is excluded because it can contain scripts.)</li>
        <li><code>documents()</code> – PDF, plain text and Word documents.</li>
        <li><code>accept('application/zip', 'image/*')</code> – any MIME types; wildcards like <code>image/*</code> are supported. Calls accumulate, so <code>images()-&gt;accept('application/pdf')</code> allows both.</li>
        <li><code>acceptAny()</code> – explicitly allow every type. Executable extensions such as <code>.php</code> are still never preserved.</li>
        <li><code>maxSize('5MB')</code> – per-file limit. Accepts bytes (<code>5242880</code>) or strings like <code>'512KB'</code>, <code>'5MB'</code>, <code>'1GB'</code>. Defaults to 5 MB.</li>
        <li><code>to('/absolute/path')</code> – store somewhere other than <code>storage/uploads</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <strong>Allowed types are required.</strong> Calling <code>store()</code> without <code>images()</code>, <code>documents()</code>,
        <code>accept()</code> or <code>acceptAny()</code> throws a <code>LogicException</code>, so you can never accidentally accept everything.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Note:</strong> your PHP limits (<code>upload_max_filesize</code> and <code>post_max_size</code> in <code>php.ini</code>)
        apply before Rhapsody sees the file. When a request exceeds <code>post_max_size</code> the uploader reports it clearly.
    </p>

    <h2>The Result Object</h2>
    <p><code>store()</code> returns an immutable <code>UploadResult</code>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;ok()</code> / <code>$result-&gt;failed()</code> – true when every file was stored and nothing went wrong.</li>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;files</code> – list of <code>UploadedFile</code> objects (<code>originalName</code>, <code>filename</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>mimeType</code>).</li>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;first()</code> – the first stored file, handy for single-file fields.</li>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;errors</code> – messages keyed by the client's original filename. Problems not tied to one file (nothing sent, request too large) are under <code>_request</code>.</li>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;firstError()</code> – the first error message, or <code>null</code>.</li>
        <li><code>$result-&gt;toArray()</code> – a JSON-ready payload that never includes absolute server paths.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When some files succeed and others fail, the successful files stay stored and <code>ok()</code> returns false. Use the <code>path</code> property of an <code>UploadedFile</code> when you need its location on the server:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$result = new FileUploader($request)-&gt;images()-&gt;maxSize('2MB')-&gt;store('avatar');

if ($result-&gt;failed()) {
    return $this-&gt;json(['error' =&gt; $result-&gt;firstError()], 422);
}

$avatar = $result-&gt;first();   // UploadedFile
$user-&gt;setAvatar($avatar-&gt;filename);</code></pre>
    <p>The JSON returned by <code>toArray()</code> looks like this:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-json">{
    "success": false,
    "files": [
        { "original_name": "cat.png", "filename": "9f2c…e1.png", "size": 48213, "mime_type": "image/png" }
    ],
    "errors": {
        "notes.exe": ["Files of type application/x-dosexec are not allowed."]
    }
}</code></pre>

    <h2>The Front End</h2>
    <p>
        Include <code>public/js/rhapsody-uploader.js</code> and add <code>data-rhapsody-upload</code> (the endpoint URL) to any form that
        contains a file input. Put <code>csrf_field()</code> inside the form; the script sends the <code>_token</code> value with every file.
    </p>
    {% verbatim %}
    <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;form data-rhapsody-upload="{{ base_url }}/upload"&gt;
    {{ csrf_field() }}
    &lt;input type="file" name="documents" multiple required&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Upload&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;ul data-upload-list&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

&lt;script src="{{ base_url }}/js/rhapsody-uploader.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
    {% endverbatim %}
    <p>
        The <code>&lt;ul data-upload-list&gt;</code> is optional; it is created automatically if you leave it out. A complete, styled example
        lives in <code>views/upload.twig</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>How it works</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>The script intercepts <code>submit</code> and sends each file in its own <code>XMLHttpRequest</code>. XHR is used because, unlike <code>fetch()</code>, it reports upload progress. A separate request per file also means each file gets its own progress bar and error message.</li>
        <li>The submit button is disabled while uploads are running, and the progress list is announced to screen readers (<code>aria-live</code>).</li>
        <li>Server errors are read from the JSON response (<code>errors[filename]</code>), so the messages from <code>UploadResult</code> appear next to the right file.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Styling hooks</h3>
    <p>
        The script injects no CSS and uses the native <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> element. Style it with attribute selectors:
        <code>li[data-state="waiting|uploading|success|error"]</code>, <code>[data-role="name"]</code>, <code>progress</code> and <code>[data-role="status"]</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Events</h3>
    <p>Events bubble from the form, so you can react to uploads without touching the script:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>rhapsody:upload:start</code> – <code>detail.files</code></li>
        <li><code>rhapsody:upload:success</code> – <code>detail.file</code>, <code>detail.response</code></li>
        <li><code>rhapsody:upload:error</code> – <code>detail.file</code>, <code>detail.message</code>, <code>detail.response</code></li>
        <li><code>rhapsody:upload:complete</code> – <code>detail.results</code></li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code class="language-javascript">document.querySelector('form[data-rhapsody-upload]')
    .addEventListener('rhapsody:upload:success', (e) =&gt; {
        console.log('Stored:', e.detail.response.files);
    });</code></pre>

    <h2>Security Notes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The MIME type is detected from the file's contents (<code>finfo</code>), not from the browser-supplied type.</li>
        <li>Stored filenames are random and extensions come from the detected type, so an uploaded <code>shell.php</code> can never be saved as executable code.</li>
        <li>Uploads go to <code>storage/uploads</code>, outside the web root. Serve them through a controller rather than linking to them directly.</li>
        <li>Keep the upload route behind CSRF protection. The JavaScript sends the token for you.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Upgrading from the Old API</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><code>new FileUploader()</code> → <code>new FileUploader($request)</code></li>
        <li><code>setAllowedMimes([...])</code> → <code>accept(...)</code>, <code>images()</code> or <code>documents()</code> (now required)</li>
        <li><code>setMaxSize($bytes)</code> → <code>maxSize('5MB')</code></li>
        <li><code>handle('field')</code> → <code>store('field')</code>, which returns an <code>UploadResult</code></li>
        <li><code>getErrors()</code> / <code>getUploadedFiles()</code> → <code>$result-&gt;errors</code> / <code>$result-&gt;files</code></li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}
